\nwhere \\(P_a\\) is the distribution used to assess the hypothetical action. The disagreement between decision theories concerns how to construct \\(P_a\\), not necessarily what counts as utility.\n\n**Evidential decision theory (EDT)** uses ordinary conditional probability:\n\n\\[\nQ_{\\mathrm{EDT}}(a)\n=\\mathbb E[U\\mid X=x,A=a],\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(X=x\\) is the observation history and \\(A\\) the physical action. It asks what news the action would provide about the outcome.\n\n**Causal decision theory (CDT)** instead evaluates a causal intervention:\n\n\\[\nQ_{\\mathrm{CDT}}(a)\n=\\mathbb E[U\\mid \\operatorname{do}(A=a),X=x].\n\\]\n\nIn a suitable causal model, setting \\(A\\) severs its normal causes and propagates the change through its causal descendants. A past prediction is not ordinarily a causal descendant of the present act.\n\nA-FDT intervenes at a different location. Let\n\n\\[\nD_{P,G,x}\n\\]\n\nbe a variable *in the agent’s model* representing the output of A-FDT itself when supplied with probability model \\(P\\), dependency graph \\(G\\), and observation history \\(x\\). The paper’s Equation (4), using this notation, is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\operatorname{A\\!-\\!FDT}(P,G,x)\n\\in\n\\arg\\max_{a\\in\\mathcal A}\n\\mathbb E_P\n\\!\\left[\nU\\mid\\operatorname{do}(D_{P,G,x}=a)\n\\right].\n}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe variable \\(D_{P,G,x}\\) must be distinguished from the actual output \\(\\operatorname{A\\!-\\!FDT}(P,G,x)\\). The first is a model node manipulated to construct hypotheticals; the second is the action returned after evaluating them.\n\nThe graph \\(G\\) is also broader than an ordinary causal graph. It is meant to encode relevant **subjunctive dependencies**: relationships through which other variables would vary with the output of the decision procedure. These include causal effects, but may also include a predictor’s simulation or another implementation of the same computation.\n\nEquation (1) supplies a rule *given* such a model. It does not, by itself, explain how to learn every relevant dependency or how to represent all counterfactuals about mathematical computations. Those are central open problems acknowledged by the paper.\n\n## 2. Subjunctive dependence and counterpossibilities\n\nImagine two correctly functioning calculators computing the same sum. Their outputs match, not because one calculator sends a signal to the other, but because each computes the same mathematical function on the same input. Similarly, two agents implementing the same decision procedure on the same relevant input may reach matching decisions without communicating.\n\nThe paper calls this kind of connection *subjunctive dependence*. A predictor need not literally run an identical program: a sufficiently accurate simulation, proof, or other model may make its prediction depend on what the agent’s procedure returns. Conversely, correlation alone is not enough. Two calculators may happen to be painted pink without either color depending on the other.\n\nThese hypotheticals are unusual. If a deterministic function has a particular output on a particular input, it cannot literally have a different output on that same input. Thus an alternative in Equation (1) may be a **counterpossibility**: a hypothetical whose antecedent is mathematically false.\n\nA-FDT does not say that an agent physically changes arithmetic, sends information into the past, or believes a false mathematical claim. The hypotheticals are instruments for comparing decisions. The model is deliberately modified at one node, and the dependencies represented by the remaining structure determine what else changes. As with an ordinary structural intervention, this requires a choice about which equations to hold fixed; unlike the ordinary causal case, the justification for those choices is not yet a settled general theory.\n\nSelf-reference is involved, but it is not automatically fatal. A program can contain a description naming itself without first knowing its own output. The paper points to standard self-reference constructions as a way to make such naming coherent. That observation does not solve the harder problem of specifying the correct counterpossible graph.\n\n## 3. Newcomb’s problem\n\nIn Newcomb’s problem, a transparent box contains \\$1,000. An opaque box contains \\$1 million if a predictor predicted that the agent would take only the opaque box, and otherwise contains nothing. The predictor has already acted.\n\nFor a simple A-FDT model, let \\(D\\in\\{1,2\\}\\) denote the decision output, with \\(1\\) meaning one-box and \\(2\\) meaning two-box. Assume:\n\n1. The physical action equals \\(D\\).\n2. The predictor correctly predicts \\(D\\) with probability \\(p\\).\n3. The predictor’s error mechanism has the same probability under both interventions.\n\nThe graph has arrows from \\(D\\) to both the physical action and the prediction, then from prediction to box contents. For \\(p=0.99\\),\n\n\\[\nQ(1)=0.99(1{,}000{,}000)=\\$990{,}000,\n\\]\n\nwhereas\n\n\\[\nQ(2)=0.01(1{,}000{,}000)+1{,}000\n=\\$11{,}000.\n\\]\n\nA-FDT one-boxes. More generally, under these symmetric-error assumptions it one-boxes exactly when\n\n\\[\np(1{,}000{,}000)>\n(1-p)(1{,}000{,}000)+1{,}000,\n\\]\n\nor \\(p>0.5005\\).\n\nThe key assumption is not merely that past predictions were 99% accurate on average. For the calculation, the model must say how prediction changes under *each decision-output intervention*. Aggregate accuracy over a different population, or accuracy concentrated on one action, does not establish those two counterfactual probabilities.\n\nCDT two-boxes in the usual model: holding the already-filled box fixed, the extra \\$1,000 is free. Standard EDT often one-boxes, because one-boxing is evidence that the box is full. A-FDT agrees with EDT’s conclusion here but arrives at it differently: it preserves the modelled dependence between the predictor and the decision output rather than conditioning on the bodily act as evidence.\n\nThere is no backward physical causation. Under the one-box hypothetical, the predictor’s earlier model yields a one-box prediction; under the two-box hypothetical, that model yields a different prediction. The intervention is on a represented computation, not on a physical event after the prediction.\n\n## 4. Why the smoking lesion is different\n\nIn the imaginary smoking-lesion problem, a lesion both makes smoking attractive and raises the probability of fatal cancer. Smoking itself has no causal effect on cancer. Suppose an agent values smoking at \\$1,000 and survival at \\$1 million.\n\nA simple EDT model might make smoking strong evidence of the lesion, and hence of cancer. It can therefore recommend refraining merely because refraining is better news. CDT smokes: changing the physical act does not change the lesion or cancer.\n\nA-FDT also smokes in the paper’s model, but for a more specific reason. The lesion and cancer do not depend on the output of the relevant decision computation. Thus, if \\(s\\) and \\(r\\) mean smoke and refrain, and the same decision node controls the action in both hypotheticals,\n\n\\[\nQ(s)-Q(r)=\\$1{,}000.\n\\]\n\nIn the paper’s more elaborate model, the lesion can determine which of two decision computations is implemented, because the two possible utility functions value smoking differently. Intervening on the smoking-valuing computation changes the act only in the fraction \\(p\\) of cases where that computation controls it. The difference is then \\(p\\cdot \\$1{,}000\\), still positive for \\(p>0\\).\n\nThe contrast with Newcomb is structural. A predictor represents the decision; a lesion is not thereby evaluating it. This does not mean that biological conditions can never affect reasoning. It means that, in this stipulated model, intervening on the decision output does not alter the lesion’s propensity to cause cancer.\n\nThe paper itself cautions that the smoking-lesion setup becomes contrived when one models an agent’s knowledge of its own preferences and decision procedure. Some versions of EDT can also avoid the supposed mistake by conditioning on sufficiently informative evidence about the agent’s desire to smoke. The example illustrates a distinction between prediction and common-cause correlation; it is not an unconditional proof that every EDT agent refuses to smoke.\n\n## 5. Observations and transparent Newcomb\n\nThe observation argument \\(x\\) in Equation (1) has an important role. A-FDT responds to a different observation by intervening on a *different decision node*. It does **not**, as written, add an ordinary condition \\(X=x\\) to the expected utility on the right-hand side.\n\nConsider transparent Newcomb. The predictor fills the million-dollar box if it predicts that the agent would one-box *upon seeing the box full*. The agent now sees a full box. Let\n\n- \\(D_F\\): the decision output on seeing a full box;\n- \\(D_E\\): the decision output on seeing an empty box.\n\nAssume the predictor predicts \\(D_F\\) correctly with probability \\(0.99\\), and let \\(q\\) be the probability that \\(D_E\\) two-boxes. When evaluating \\(D_F=\\text{one-box}\\), the model gives\n\n\\[\nQ_F(\\text{one})\n=0.99(1{,}000{,}000)+0.01q(1{,}000).\n\\]\n\nWhen evaluating \\(D_F=\\text{two-box}\\), it gives\n\n\\[\nQ_F(\\text{two})\n=0.01(1{,}001{,}000)+0.99q(1{,}000).\n\\]\n\nThe first is larger for every \\(q\\in[0,1]\\). Hence, on seeing the full box, A-FDT outputs one-box.\n\nWhy does the second hypothetical include mostly empty-box cases, when the agent *actually sees* a full box? Because changing \\(D_F\\) changes what the predictor predicts. In most of that hypothetical distribution, the predictor leaves the box empty, and the agent runs \\(D_E\\), not \\(D_F\\). The actual observation identifies the computation being evaluated; it is not held fixed throughout the intervention.\n\nThis is a strong and controversial feature, not an incidental notational choice. A reader might reasonably object that, once the box is visibly full, the only decision-relevant difference is the extra \\$1,000. That is the CDT-style assessment the authors reject. Their rationale is that a reliably predicted disposition to take the extra money generally results in seeing an empty box.\n\nThe paper’s transparent-Newcomb calculation writes an expression that appears to include an observation inside the intervention, but its subsequent numerical calculation allows the observation to change. Equation (1), together with that calculation, supports the latter interpretation. Automatically conditioning on the full-box observation would instead eliminate the main effect responsible for its stated answer.\n\n## 6. Threats, twins, and conditional actions\n\nThe same distinction applies to **Parfit’s hitchhiker**. A driver rescues an agent only if she predicts that the agent will pay \\$1,000 upon reaching town. The paper’s A-FDT agent pays after arrival: the relevant output is the decision computation used on reaching town, and the rescue decision depends on the driver’s prediction of that output.\n\nA related example is mechanical blackmail. Suppose a blackmailer starts a damaging program if she predicts the agent would pay \\$1,000 on receiving a warning. If the agent refuses after a warning, the program causes \\$1 million in damage. Under a perfect predictor, the A-FDT hypothetical for refusal says that the warning is never sent; the hypothetical for payment says that it is sent and paid. Thus A-FDT refuses even if the warning is actually received.\n\nImperfect prediction makes the result quantitative. As an additional simplified model, suppose payment leads to a warning and a \\$1,000 loss, while refusal leads to an erroneous warning with probability \\(\\varepsilon\\) and a \\$1 million loss when warned. Then\n\n\\[\nQ(\\text{pay})=-1{,}000,\\qquad\nQ(\\text{refuse})=-1{,}000{,}000\\varepsilon.\n\\]\n\nRefusal is preferred when \\(\\varepsilon<0.001\\). The threshold changes if there are other costs, asymmetric errors, or ways to stop the program. A-FDT is not a rule to refuse every threat: its answer depends on the modelled effect of the decision output on the threat and on the resulting utilities.\n\nIn a prisoner’s dilemma against a perfect computational twin, the relevant dependence can instead connect two simultaneous actions. If both implement the same decision computation on the same input, intervening on its output changes both actions. With payoffs of \\$1 million for mutual cooperation and \\$1,000 for mutual defection, A-FDT cooperates. It does not follow that one should cooperate with every similar person. Different information, incentives, code, or random seeds can weaken or remove the dependency.\n\n## 7. How to build a model for an unfamiliar case\n\nEquation (1) becomes usable only after a model has been supplied. The following is an **operational proposal**, not a completed construction provided by the paper.\n\nFirst, identify the precise decision computation and its input. If the question is what to do after receiving a warning, represent the output for that observation separately from outputs for other observations. If the agent could learn additional facts before acting, include the corresponding decision nodes.\n\nSecond, represent physical outcomes and ordinary causal mechanisms: actions, costs, predictions, observations, and other agents’ acts. Include uncertainty about relevant exogenous factors.\n\nThird, add a dependency from the selected decision-output node to another variable only when there is a substantive reason to think that variable tracks the computation’s output under the contemplated intervention. Exact shared computation is a clear idealized case; a predictor’s simulation may support a noisy dependency. A shared background cause or observed correlation, by itself, is not enough.\n\nFourth, for each candidate \\(a\\), replace the selected node’s equation with \\(D_{P,G,x}=a\\). Propagate changes through the stipulated structural equations and calculate expected utility over the *whole* resulting distribution, including branches in which \\(x\\) is not observed. Keep unrelated variables and noise distributions fixed unless the model provides a reason not to.\n\nFinally, compare values and test sensitivity to uncertain assumptions. In particular, vary predictor error rates, the extent of similarity between agents, and the possibility that the observation itself depends on the decision. If the preferred action changes across plausible models, the source does not supply a unique answer merely by naming A-FDT.\n\nThis procedure also has limitations. A graphical edge is not a proof of a genuine logical dependence; an incorrectly drawn edge can manufacture a desired answer. Nor does ordinary statistical estimation always identify what a predictor would have done under a false mathematical antecedent. One practical response is to maintain a distribution over plausible dependency models and average their intervention values, but that is an additional modelling choice requiring its own justification.\n\n## 8. Actions, policies, and the limits of the claims\n\nA **policy** is a mapping \\(\\pi:x\\mapsto a\\) from possible observations to actions. The paper says its authors prefer a formulation that selects policies, especially for some multi-agent cases, but Equation (1) selects an action for a particular input. It is therefore unsafe simply to substitute “choose the best policy” for A-FDT’s action rule.\n\nA policy-level proposal might introduce a node \\(\\Pi\\) and compare\n\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[U\\mid\\operatorname{do}(\\Pi=\\pi)]\n\\]\n\nover available policies. But one would still have to specify which policies are candidates, how other agents predict them, and how that intervention relates to the separate nodes \\(D_{P,G,x}\\). A one-node action intervention can leave other observation-contingent decisions unchanged; a policy intervention can change several at once. They need not agree.\n\nThe paper argues that A-FDT often behaves as an agent would have wished to precommit: paying the driver, for example, makes rescue possible. It also argues that information need not have negative value, since an agent can disregard an unhelpful signal. These are motivations and claims about the intended theory, not general theorems established by Equation (1) alone. Policy availability, the modelling of information, and dependencies between successive computations all matter.\n\nNor is universal superiority possible. An environment can reward agents specifically for following a rival decision theory. The authors propose informally comparing theories on “fair” problems, where outcomes depend on behavior in the present problem rather than on the name of the procedure, but acknowledge that a satisfactory formal definition of fairness is open. Their examples support a case for A-FDT; they do not prove it optimal across all environments.\n\n**Reference:** Eliezer Yudkowsky and Nate Soares, *Functional Decision Theory: A New Theory of Instrumental Rationality*, especially Sections 3–6 and Equation (4), [arXiv:1710.05060v2](https://arxiv.org/abs/1710.05060v2).\n\n## Conclusion\n\nA-FDT evaluates an act by intervening on the output of the decision procedure that produces it, then propagating the change through relevant causal and computational dependencies. This explains both its attraction—taking predictors and copies seriously—and its difficulty: the correct structure of those dependencies is not supplied by ordinary probability alone. Given an explicit model, Equation (1) can yield precise answers; constructing and justifying that model remains part of the research problem.","refusal":null,"reasoning":null}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":56606,"completion_tokens":4037,"total_tokens":60643,"cost":0.02301815,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":56603,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.02301815,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.00283315,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.020185},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":0,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}